A||||4||The Aura||Spring rubbers||||5||
Z||000000||The Aura||11-21-2001||03:09 PM||||It might sound kind of mickey mouse, but is it possible to run the front springs without the upper spring rubber to get a little extra lowering of the car (i would guess an extra 1/2". Has anybody done this?<P>------------------<BR>1986 GT 4spd&gt; Suspension Techniques Springs, Poly Bushings, Bosal Brospeed 4.5" Exhaust Tips||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tigger||11-21-2001||03:16 PM||||No, don't think anyone has.  I'm sure your car would get noisy, those isolators do a lot to dampen the spring and keep it from turning in the seat.  Without it you would probably get a good "Blang" whenever hitting a bump.  Could even cause a coil to break if it has a weak spot.  I would say cutting the springs back would be "better" way than to remove the rubber isolator.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-21-2001||06:10 PM||||I think he means the rubbers on the springs. not the bump stoppers.<P>The rubbers on the springs them selves isolate allot of creaking and groaning caused by the springs as they do their job. <P>They can also prevent the spring from grinding against the seat. (The wire can twist and move some as the spring is compressed.)<P>Depending on the installation, they also help keep the spring from turning in the seats. I'm not sure if that's an issue on Fiero or not.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||11-23-2001||12:58 PM||||There are other kinds of isolators that are thinner,work better by letting the spring do all the movement.I've used some older Fiat ones,as they were a plastic material with a thin metal shell against the spring.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eriamjh||11-23-2001||01:23 PM||||I'm ripping apart my Fiero's suspension and just pulled both front spings.<P>First: I don't think there is any way tha the springs will turn in their seats.  The lower ctrl arm has a divet in it that the end of the spring sits in.  This will at least prevent the spring from turning one way.  <P>As for the other way, these things are under a ton of compression.  I don't see where the forces would come from to rotate the spring, but I guess it could happen.  The upper spring (rubber) insulator) is designed to fit the end of the spring and evenly fill in the gap at the top.<P>Without it, I think you'll get some metal-to-metal wear in the upper spring seat.  There could be some squeaking and even grinding sounds coming out without it.<P>If you're gonna go through all the trouble to pull the spring to remove that insulator, you might as well just replace the spring.<P>By the way: Does anyone know where you can get those spring insulators?||||reg||1
